\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Manhattan Beach Rotary Club Member Scott Yanofsky presented a YouTube video highlighting the 2024 5th Annual VetFest and invited the community to attend the upcoming 6th Annual VetFest, which will be held at Kinecta Federal Credit Union on Saturday, November 8, 2025, from 12:00 PM to 5:00 PM.\cf1\b\i0\line\line\cf0\b0\i Police Captain Matt Sabosky invited the community to attend the Tip-A-Cop event at Tin Roof Bistro on Wednesday, November 5, 2025, from 5:00 PM to 8:00 PM. Proceeds from the event will benefit local Special Olympics athletes.\line\line Waste Management Recycling Education Representative Bobby Bell provided an update on the recent composting class, noting it was a great success and that all compost bins were sold out. He also reported that approximately 170 residents and commercial businesses participated in the Free Paper Shredding event held this past Saturday, where documents were securely shredded and recycled.\line\line Library Manager Cassandra Sargent invited the community to attend the upcoming speaker series featuring best-selling author Nic Stone on Saturday, October 25, 2025, at 2:00 PM.\line\line City Manager Talyn Mirzakhanian provided an update on the Scout House Project, noting that the applicant, The Friends Group in collaboration with Tomaro Architecture, has submitted building plans to the City\rquote s Building and Safety Division, initiating the formal plan check process. The plans are currently under concurrent review by Building and Safety, Planning, Fire, and Public Works staff, with the first round of review expected to conclude by the end of November. Corrections will then be issued to the applicant, who has committed to promptly addressing them. Given the project\rquote s complexity, the full plan check process is expected to take 6-12 months. Staff anticipates returning to City Council with a final cost estimate once the review is complete, and if the project is fully funded, a notice to proceed with construction could be issued in late 2026.\line\line Councilmember Tarnay took a moment to honor the life of Ryan James, a beloved member of the Manhattan Beach community and Mira Costa High School alumnus. Ryan, a freshman at UCLA, tragically passed away last week following an epileptic seizure. He was a dedicated leader and volunteer, actively involved with the South Bay Community Coalition Against Hate, The Friendship Foundation, Special Olympics, and the Mira Costa Cross Country and Track teams. In his memory, his family encourages the community to raise awareness about epilepsy and support efforts toward finding a cure.\line\line\pard Mayor Lesser provided an update on the recent Chevron Refinery fire, noting that the City of Manhattan Beach, along with regional partners including the City of El Segundo and Chevron, is reviewing communication protocols to improve emergency messaging. Chevron has committed to enhancing its public outreach efforts. Several City Councilmembers and the Mayor visited the incident site to better understand the event and ongoing investigation. He also announced that a Town Hall Meeting focused on emergency preparedness is being planned for early next year. The public is encouraged to sign up for Alert SouthBay emergency notifications by texting MBALERTS to 888777 or visiting alertsouthbay.com. Additionally, the Mayor invited the community to attend Coffee with the Mayor at Metlox Plaza on November 7, 2025, from 9:00 AM to 10:00 AM.\cf2\i0\f1\par
}


