1.. Presentation of a Proclamation Declaring October 19-25, 2025, as United Against Hate Week.
PROCLAIM {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss\fprq2\fcharset0 Arial;}{\f1\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue255;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Former Mayor Hildy Stern thanked the City Council for supporting the proclamation, noting that it aligns Manhattan Beach with other coastal cities participating in the United Against Hate initiative. She shared that the movement began in 2017 in the San Francisco Bay Area in response to a white supremacist protest and has since grown from about 200 communities to a nationwide effort. She also noted that in October 2022, the South Bay Community Coalition Against Hate was formed by the Manhattan Beach City Council and has expanded quickly.\line\line Manhattan Beach Unified School District Board Member Tina Shivpuri shared several events taking place throughout the week, including a \ldblquote Yoga for Unity\rdblquote  event held the previous evening at AdventurePlex in partnership with the Beach Cities Health District. She also announced a Community by Candlelight gathering scheduled for tomorrow at 6:30 PM in the City of El Segundo, an Interfaith Unity Walk on Thursday morning starting at the Sepulveda Overpass on the Greenbelt, and noted that 16 locations will be participating in activities throughout the week, including a food drive benefiting local nonprofits such as The Single Mom Project.\line\line Manhattan Beach Unified School District Board Member Jen Dohner shared that the locations for the food drive can be found on the website southbayvshate.org. She also introduced a new awareness poster that includes a resource phone number 211, for individuals who are victims of hate incidents, hate crimes, or bullying.\cf1\i0\line\line\cf0\i On behalf of the City Council, Mayor Lesser presented a Proclamation declaring October 19-25, 2025, as United Against Hate Week.\cf2\i0\f1\par
